/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  direct_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  /* Player 1, pinned. Everything else is measured relative to this origin. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    number_of_tiles 200
    base_size 5
    clumping_factor 20
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 50
    assign_to AT_PLAYER 1 0 0
  }

  /* TEST NEAR - 30 tiles north of player 1. Inside the claimed 45-tile band. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    number_of_tiles 100
    base_size 3
    clumping_factor 50
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 35
    zone 40
  }

  /* TEST FAR - 60 tiles north of player 1. Outside the claimed 45-tile band. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT2
    number_of_tiles 100
    base_size 3
    clumping_factor 50
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 20
    zone 41
  }

  /* CONTROL NEAR - 30 tiles south. Same shape, the KNOWN-good attribute. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT3
    number_of_tiles 100
    base_size 3
    clumping_factor 50
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 65
    zone 42
  }

  /* CONTROL FAR - 60 tiles south. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DESERT
    number_of_tiles 100
    base_size 3
    clumping_factor 50
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 80
    zone 43
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  /* TEST NEAR. If temp_min_distance_to_players works like the real
     attribute, this places NOTHING - the marker is only 30 tiles out. */
  create_object STONE
  {
    number_of_objects 20
    set_gaia_object_only
    terrain_to_place_on DIRT
    temp_min_distance_to_players 45
  }

  /* TEST FAR. Outside the band either way - should always place in full. */
  create_object GOLD
  {
    number_of_objects 20
    set_gaia_object_only
    terrain_to_place_on DIRT2
    temp_min_distance_to_players 45
  }

  /* CONTROL NEAR, same shape, the attribute already confirmed frameless
     (docs/build-log.md, "frameless min_distance_to_players pinned"). Must
     place NOTHING, or the harness itself - not the tested attribute - is
     what's broken. */
  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 20
    set_gaia_object_only
    terrain_to_place_on DIRT3
    min_distance_to_players 45
  }

  /* CONTROL FAR. Must place in full, or the harness is broken. */
  create_object CYPRESS_TREE
  {
    number_of_objects 20
    set_gaia_object_only
    terrain_to_place_on DESERT
    min_distance_to_players 45
  }


/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_67 - does temp_min_distance_to_players do anything, and if so what
docs/known-issues.md BUG-019, de-official-map-issues.md Sec.4
(real_world_world.rms:498), reference/data/language.json (create_object
attributes)
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
real_world_world.rms writes `temp_min_distance_to_players 45`, a name with no
entry in language.json and no closer match than min_distance_to_players (a
real attribute) and temp_min_distance_group_placement (a real, differently-
scoped "temp_"-prefixed attribute, so the naming pattern is not itself
unprecedented). Two readings, neither confirmed: (a) an author typo for
min_distance_to_players; (b) a genuine undocumented sibling that behaves the
same way. Per CLAUDE.md's rule that the guide has to license a reading with a
sentence, not a naming resemblance, this cannot be decided by looking at the
name - it has to change generation, and change it the SAME way
min_distance_to_players does, before language.json gets touched.

METHOD - fixed geometry, not luck
direct_placement pins player 1 at (100,100); players 2+ get no land, which is
intentional and removes every other player origin from the picture. Four
small marker lands sit at fixed, forced offsets - 30 tiles (inside the 45-tile
band the real map declares) and 60 tiles (outside it) - one pair testing the
attribute under question, one pair testing the KNOWN attribute as a control
for the harness itself. Each marker carries a distinct terrain, and each
paired gaia object is restricted with `terrain_to_place_on` to ONLY its own
marker, so a placed/not-placed read is unambiguous - no distance math on the
object positions is needed, just a headcount.

READ CONTROL FIRST. It has to come back clean before the TEST arms mean
anything - same discipline BUG-022's RMSTEST_64 used ("read arms 1 and 7
against each other first").

  FORAGE (control near, 30 tiles)   expect 0    if nonzero, the harness itself
                                                 is broken (marker too far/near,
                                                 or the frameless reading of
                                                 min_distance_to_players itself
                                                 needs revisiting) - STOP, do
                                                 not read the TEST arms yet
  CYPRESS_TREE (control far, 60 tiles) expect 20  if not 20, same stop

Once the control reads 0 / 20:

  STONE (test near, 30 tiles)   0    the attribute enforces the same band as
                                      min_distance_to_players - it is reading
                                      (b), a genuine sibling, and should be
                                      added to language.json with the same
                                      semantics as min_distance_to_players
                 20 (same as unrestricted)   the attribute does nothing - this
                                      is reading (a) or a no-op sibling either
                                      way, and language.json gets an ALIAS
                                      entry (or nothing at all) rather than a
                                      distance-checking one
  GOLD (test far, 60 tiles)     expect 20 regardless of which reading holds -
                                      it is a redundant confirmation, since
                                      outside the band both readings agree.
                                      If it comes back below 20, something
                                      about the marker/terrain setup is wrong
                                      and the whole run needs a second look.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), 8 PLAYERS, ALL UN-TEAMED (plain FFA - no lobby
needed; only player 1's land exists, same as RMSTEST_37). ONE RUN - every
outcome here is a presence/absence at a fixed geometry, not a statistical
question, same shape as RMSTEST_42's single-run read for BUG-007.

If any of the four marker terrains is missing from the terrain histogram
entirely, that land failed to grow rather than being distance-limited -
regenerate before reading anything.
================================================================================
*/
